---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import FlowerSim from '../../simulators/flower/FlowerSim.astro';
import { plantReproductionQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/plant-reproduction';

const toc = [
  { id: 'two-steps', label: 'Pollination is not fertilisation' },
  { id: 'parts', label: 'The reproductive parts' },
  { id: 'pollen-ovule', label: 'Inside a pollen grain and an ovule' },
  { id: 'carriers', label: 'Animal and wind pollination' },
  { id: 'self-cross', label: 'Self- and cross-pollination' },
  { id: 'tube', label: 'Germination and the pollen tube' },
  { id: 'double', label: 'Double fertilisation' },
  { id: 'seed-fruit', label: 'From ovule to seed, ovary to fruit' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Why it matters' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the simulator simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Chapter 32: Plant Reproduction. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/32-introduction' },
  { text: 'Raven, P. H., Evert, R. F., & Eichhorn, S. E. (2013). Biology of Plants (8th ed.). W. H. Freeman.' },
  { text: 'Dresselhaus, T., Sprunck, S., & Wessel, G. M. (2016). Fertilization mechanisms in flowering plants. Current Biology, 26(3), R125–R139.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2015.12.032' },
  { text: 'Takayama, S., & Isogai, A. (2005). Self-incompatibility in plants. Annual Review of Plant Biology, 56, 467–489.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.arplant.56.032604.144249' },
  { text: 'Ollerton, J., Winfree, R., & Tarrant, S. (2011). How many flowering plants are pollinated by animals? Oikos, 120(3), 321–326.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1600-0706.2010.18644.x' },
  { text: 'Klein, A.-M., et al. (2007). Importance of pollinators in changing landscapes for world crops. Proceedings of the Royal Society B, 274, 303–313.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2006.3721' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="botany"
  topic="plant-reproduction"
  description="Interactive flower reproduction simulator: explore a 3D flower, carry pollen by hand, bee or wind, compare self- and cross-pollination, and follow pollen tube growth, double fertilisation, and seed and fruit development."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={plantReproductionQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Every apple, grain of rice and sunflower seed starts when a pollen grain lands on the right flower. But landing is only
      the beginning: the pollen must grow a tube, deliver two sperm cells, and trigger a double fertilisation that only
      flowering plants perform. Take a flower apart in 3D, move pollen between plants, and follow the journey all the way to
      seed and fruit.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <FlowerSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>In <strong>3D flower</strong>, drag to rotate and tap a part to learn its job. Turn on <strong>Cut the flower in half</strong> to see the ovules inside the ovary.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Pollination</strong>, drag pollen from an anther onto a stigma. Compare the same flower, the other plant, and the blue flower of another species. Then let a <strong>bee</strong> or the <strong>wind</strong> do the work.</li>
      <li>Switch <strong>Self-incompatible plants</strong> off and on to see when self-pollination can and cannot lead to seeds.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Fertilisation</strong>, press <strong>Start</strong> or <strong>Next stage</strong> to follow one pollen grain through seven stages, with a magnified view of each.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset</strong> restores every setting.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="two-steps">Pollination is not fertilisation</h2>
  <p>
    These two words are often mixed up, but they describe different events, separated by hours or even days.
    <strong>Pollination</strong> is the transfer of pollen from an anther to a stigma. <strong>Fertilisation</strong> is the
    fusion of a sperm cell with an egg cell, deep inside an ovule. Between them, the pollen grain must germinate and grow a
    pollen tube all the way down the style. Pollination can happen without fertilisation, for example when pollen lands on a
    flower of the wrong species.
  </p>

  <h2 id="parts">The reproductive parts</h2>
  <p>
    A flower’s male parts are the <strong>stamens</strong>: an <strong>anther</strong>, which makes pollen, on a stalk called
    the <strong>filament</strong>. Its female part is the <strong>carpel</strong> (or pistil): a sticky
    <strong>stigma</strong> that catches pollen, a <strong>style</strong>, and an <strong>ovary</strong> containing one or more
    <strong>ovules</strong>. Petals attract pollinators and sepals protect the bud. You can explore these parts, and the
    tissues of the whole plant, in the <a href="/botany/plant-anatomy">Plant Anatomy simulator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pollen-ovule">Inside a pollen grain and an ovule</h2>
  <p>
    A pollen grain is not a sperm. It is a tiny <strong>male gametophyte</strong> of two or three cells inside a tough wall:
    a large <strong>tube cell</strong> and a small <strong>generative cell</strong>, which divides by mitosis to make
    <strong>two sperm cells</strong>. An ovule contains the <strong>embryo sac</strong>, the female gametophyte. In most
    flowering plants it has seven cells and eight haploid nuclei:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the <strong>egg cell</strong> and two <strong>synergids</strong> beside it, at the end nearest the opening (the micropyle);</li>
    <li>a large <strong>central cell</strong> with two <strong>polar nuclei</strong>;</li>
    <li>three <strong>antipodal cells</strong> at the far end.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The embryo sac is wrapped in protective layers, the <strong>integuments</strong>, which will become the seed coat.</p>

  <h2 id="carriers">Animal and wind pollination</h2>
  <p>
    Roughly 88% of flowering plant species rely on animals, mostly insects, but also birds and bats. The rest, including
    grasses, cereals and many trees such as oak and birch, rely mainly on the wind.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Animal-pollinated flowers</th><th>Wind-pollinated flowers</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Large, coloured, scented petals</td><td>Small, dull flowers, often without petals</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nectar as a reward</td><td>No nectar</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sticky, spiky pollen in modest amounts</td><td>Huge amounts of light, smooth pollen</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anthers and stigma inside the flower</td><td>Dangling anthers and large, feathery stigmas</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Examples: rose, apple, sage</td><td>Examples: wheat, maize, birch</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Animal pollination is targeted: a bee that sticks to one kind of flower carries pollen to the right species. Wind is
    cheap but wasteful. In the simulator, only a few percent of wind-blown pollen ever reaches a stigma.
  </p>

  <h2 id="self-cross">Self- and cross-pollination</h2>
  <p>
    In <strong>self-pollination</strong>, pollen reaches a stigma of the same flower or another flower on the same plant. In
    <strong>cross-pollination</strong>, pollen comes from a different plant of the same species. Cross-pollination mixes the
    genes of two parents, so the offspring are more varied and better able to cope with disease and change. Self-pollination
    is reliable when pollinators are scarce, and peas, wheat and tomatoes do it routinely.
  </p>
  <p>
    Many plants, including apples, cherries and many clovers, are <strong>self-incompatible</strong>: the stigma or style
    recognises the plant’s own pollen and stops its tube from growing. That is why a lone apple tree sets little fruit
    without a second variety nearby. Other plants prevent selfing by holding the stigma above the anthers, or by ripening
    anthers and stigma at different times.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tube">Germination and the pollen tube</h2>
  <p>
    On a compatible stigma, the pollen grain absorbs water and <strong>germinates</strong>: the tube cell grows a
    <strong>pollen tube</strong> that pushes down between the cells of the style. The tube is guided by chemical signals
    from the synergids and carries the two sperm cells at its tip. Flowering-plant sperm cannot swim, so unlike mosses and
    ferns they need no water. The tube enters the ovule through the micropyle, grows into a synergid and bursts, releasing
    both sperm. Each ovule needs its own pollen tube, so a seed-packed fruit such as a tomato needs many grains of pollen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="double">Double fertilisation</h2>
  <p>Both sperm cells fuse with something, which is why the process is called <strong>double fertilisation</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sperm 1 + egg cell</strong> (n + n) → a diploid <strong>zygote</strong> (2n), which becomes the embryo.</li>
    <li><strong>Sperm 2 + central cell</strong> with its two polar nuclei (n + n + n) → a triploid <strong>primary endosperm nucleus</strong> (3n), which develops into the food store.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Double fertilisation is found in flowering plants. It means the plant invests in a food store only when an egg has
    actually been fertilised.
  </p>

  <h2 id="seed-fruit">From ovule to seed, ovary to fruit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The zygote divides by mitosis into an <strong>embryo</strong> with a root (radicle), a shoot and one or two seed leaves (cotyledons).</li>
    <li>The endosperm nucleus divides to make <strong>endosperm</strong>. In cereals it remains as the bulk of the grain (and of white flour); in beans and peas the cotyledons absorb it.</li>
    <li>The integuments harden into the <strong>seed coat</strong>. The whole <strong>ovule becomes the seed</strong>.</li>
    <li>The <strong>ovary wall becomes the fruit</strong>, which protects the seeds and helps disperse them. Petals, stamens, style and stigma wither.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Botanically, a pea pod, a tomato, a cucumber and a cherry are all fruits. What happens when a seed starts to grow is
    explored in the <a href="/botany/seed-germination">Seed Germination simulator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="applications">Why it matters</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Food</strong>: about three-quarters of the world’s leading food crops benefit from animal pollination, including apples, almonds, coffee and cocoa. Cereals such as wheat, rice and maize are wind-pollinated or self-pollinated.</li>
    <li><strong>Orchards</strong>: growers plant compatible varieties side by side and bring in honeybee hives at flowering time.</li>
    <li><strong>Plant breeding</strong>: breeders remove the anthers from a flower and dust its stigma by hand to make a chosen cross.</li>
    <li><strong>Hay fever</strong> is caused mainly by wind-blown pollen of grasses and trees, which is released in vast amounts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Pollination and fertilisation are the same thing.”</strong> Pollination moves pollen to a stigma; fertilisation is the fusion of gametes inside the ovule.</li>
    <li><strong>“A pollen grain is a sperm.”</strong> It is a tiny male gametophyte that produces two sperm cells.</li>
    <li><strong>“The sperm swims down the style.”</strong> The pollen tube grows and carries the sperm to the ovule.</li>
    <li><strong>“Fertilisation is a single fusion.”</strong> In flowering plants there are two: one forms the zygote and one forms the endosperm.</li>
    <li><strong>“The ovary becomes the seed.”</strong> Each ovule becomes a seed; the ovary becomes the fruit.</li>
    <li><strong>“Any pollen will do.”</strong> Pollen of another species, or a self-incompatible plant’s own pollen, does not lead to fertilisation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the simulator simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The flowers are generic, with four ovules in the pollination experiment and three in the long section, and nothing is
    drawn to scale. Real pollen tubes take from under an hour to several days to reach an ovule. In the experiment,
    pollen of another species never germinates; in reality some foreign pollen does germinate and is stopped later. The bee
    and wind follow simple rules, and the two plants are either fully self-incompatible or fully self-compatible. Embryo
    sacs in some species differ from the seven-celled pattern shown, and some fruits (such as strawberries) also grow from
    parts of the flower other than the ovary.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pollination is pollen transfer to a stigma; fertilisation is the fusion of sperm and egg in the ovule.</li>
    <li>Flowers are pollinated by animals or by wind, and their structure matches the carrier.</li>
    <li>Cross-pollination increases variation; self-incompatibility prevents self-fertilisation.</li>
    <li>A pollen tube delivers two sperm: one fertilises the egg (2n zygote), the other the central cell (3n endosperm).</li>
    <li>The ovule becomes the seed and the ovary becomes the fruit.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
